Description: The "Where should Wild OS go?" page and the disk editor of the Wild OS design
 The partition module's pages are widgets, so the design is built here
 rather than in QML.
 .
 Choice page (Installer.dc):
  * PrettyRadioButton can be drawn as a card by the stylesheet (a "checked"
    property for the picked one, a click anywhere picks it), and carries an
    options widget under its text, shown only while it is picked.
  * The title, a line about what is on the disk and the choices as cards in
    one column. The disk menu shows only with more than one disk. The before
    and after bars stay alive out of sight; Alongside picks the partition to
    shrink itself (the one with a system on it, else the largest that can
    shrink) and shows the splitter inside its card, with "Windows is using
    X GB of its space" and the disk's name and size under it. "Replace a
    partition" is not offered. Alongside is the first choice when possible.
    The wording names the system found ("Install alongside Windows",
    "Replace Windows with Wild OS"). An earlier Wild OS install, which
    os-prober cannot see (encrypted, or in a Btrfs subvolume), is found by
    its partition names and named on the page too.
  * alongsideBlocked in global storage (from the installer's own checks:
    "bitlocker", "faststartup", "dirty" or "space") keeps Alongside visible
    but unavailable, with the reason on its card; nothing is preselected
    then, so erasing is always a choice someone makes.
  * The plan for the summary page goes to global storage as wildosPlan.
  * PartitionSplitterWidget gains a focused mode: only the partition being
    split, as two named halves with their sizes and a white handle that can
    be dragged or moved with the arrow keys.
 .
 Disk password on a later page:
  * With luksPassphraseFrom (a global storage key) in partition.conf, the
    encryption switch is not on this page: automatic layouts and the disk
    editor make encrypted partitions with a placeholder passphrase, and
    PartitionViewStep::jobs(), which runs after every page is done, puts the
    password stored under that key in its place, or takes the encryption off
    when wildosEncrypt is false. Without a password the install stops before
    touching a disk.
 .
 BIOS computers: no boot loader menu on the cards (GRUB goes on the disk
 being installed to). Installing alongside on an MBR disk whose primary
 partitions would run out puts Wild OS in a new extended partition. The
 disk editor adds a 1 MiB bios_grub partition with the system partition on
 a GPT disk, and refuses a /boot it cannot fit on a full MBR disk.
 .
 Disk editor (InstallerManual.dc), DiskEditorPage, used instead of
 PartitionPage for "Something else":
  * A colour-coded map of the disk and a list of its partitions with plain
    names ("Windows (C:)", "Your files (Home)", "Swap") tagged Keep as is or
    New; a side panel with role buttons (Wild OS system, Your files, Swap,
    Shared with Windows, Don't use), a size slider and field for new
    partitions, Encrypt and Erase and format switches, and the device path
    in small monospace; a live "Will erase / will create" summary; "Undo all
    changes"; Add partition and Delete. The system role adds its 1 GB
    unencrypted start-up partition (/boot) itself. Continue needs a Wild OS
    system partition and a role for every new partition. Kept partitions
    only get a mount point; anything erased is made again from scratch.
Author: Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>
Forwarded: no

